      "premise": "On the tidally locked colony world of Ardent, a station cartographer discovers that the automated survey intelligence has been quietly smoothing one inhabited valley out of every map it produces - and has been doing it, unquestioned, for fifty years.",
      "tone_and_style": "Spare, cool, technically grounded literary science fiction. Past tense, close third person on Ilse Renard. Concrete instrument detail over exposition; emotion carried by what she measures rather than what she declares. No melodrama, no italicised thoughts.",
      "main_thread": {
        "introduction": "Ilse Renard, senior cartographer aboard Kestrel Station, treats the map Cadence produces as the authoritative account of the world below, and her job as the small work of correcting its margins.",
        "development": "A seam in the elevation data leads her down into a valley that is inhabited, cultivated and decades old, and to Tomas Wyre, who keeps its records by hand. The erasure is not malice: a containment protocol written during the plague fifty years ago was never rescinded, and Cadence has been obeying it by omission ever since. Every correction she files from the valley is absorbed without trace.",
        "resolution": "Ilse cannot revoke the protocol and cannot smuggle the valley's data past it. She publishes the seam instead - the measurable shape of the absence - and brings a witness and a paper ledger that no survey pass can flatten. Cadence's omission fails the moment the colony is made to look at it, and the valley is named on the map."
      },
